import { ArticleCopy } from "../../../../model/article"

pub fn copy -> ArticleCopy = ArticleCopy {
  title: "Function types and currying",
  summary: "Configure a command search with partial application, pass functions to filter and map, and read the type that remains."
}

pub fn title -> String = (copy ()).title

pub fn opening -> String =
  "The search text is \"b\" for now. We could pass it with every command name, but watch what happens when matches receives only that first argument. The search itself becomes a value we can hand to filter."

pub fn partial -> String =
  "matches takes a query, then a name, and returns Bool. matches \"b\" therefore has type String -> Bool. builds is that function, not a search result. arrays.filter uses it to decide which original labels to keep; the first output still contains the spaces and spelling from commands."

pub fn lambda -> String =
  "A function can also live right where it is needed. The lambda beginning with \\name measures each trimmed label once, so map produces an Array<Int>. There is no need to give that one-off calculation a permanent name. These lengths count Unicode scalars, not terminal display columns."

pub fn section -> String =
  "An operator can be a function value too: (+) is the curried addition function. reduce 0 (+) combines the lengths into their total, 19. This parenthesized operator reference is an operator section. Passing a function to filter, map or reduce is what makes those operations higher-order functions."

pub fn arrows -> String =
  "Function arrows group to the right: String -> String -> Bool means String -> (String -> Bool). A higher-order parameter needs its own grouping: (String -> Bool) is one predicate argument. This is part of the language semantics, not an optimization that turns an ordinary call into something different."

pub fn argumentTypes -> String =
  "Arguments arrive from the left, one at a time, and each is checked against the next parameter. Until the last argument arrives, the result is a function. The small integer program below isolates the same rule: direct and addOne 2 both produce 3. Passing a tuple with add (1, 2) instead would pass one tuple, not two Int arguments."

pub fn generic -> String =
  "A generic function can still have unresolved type parameters or trait constraints after partial application. The remaining arguments or expected result type supply the missing information. Inspect the remaining function type rather than guessing from the final value you intend to get."

pub fn unit -> String =
  "Even a declaration without named parameters takes Unit. fn answer -> Int = 42 has type Unit -> Int; answer () obtains 42, while answer by itself is still a function. Declaring the function does not run its body."

pub fn rejected -> String =
  "Here add 1 has type Int -> Int, but the annotation asks for Int, so the compiler reports SES-T0101. Add the remaining argument with add 1 2 if you need the value 3, or retain the function with let addOne = add 1 if you plan to call it later."

pub fn previousExit -> String = " — chapter start: how key is declared and called."

pub fn nextExit -> String =
  " — next: connect filtering to display, and handle a missing match."

pub fn lambdaExit -> String =
  " — look up lambda parameters and scope without reading the chapter again."

pub fn annotationsExit -> String =
  " — check where to put a type on a function-valued argument."

pub fn genericExit -> String =
  " — inspect type parameters and constraints left by partial application."

pub fn operatorExit -> String =
  " — look up which operator references are available and their fixities."
